DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssbbggeett(), ssbbsseeaarrcchh(), ssbbrreeaadd(), and ssbbffiinndd() functions provide su‐
     perblock reads for libufs(3) consumers.  The ssbbppuutt() and ssbbwwrriittee() func‐
     tions provide superblock writes for libufs(3) consumers.

     The ssbbggeett() and ssbbsseeaarrcchh() functions first allocate a buffer to hold the
     superblock.  Using the _d_e_v_f_d file descriptor that references the filesys‐
     tem disk, ssbbggeett() reads the superblock located at the byte offset speci‐
     fied by _s_b_l_o_c_k_l_o_c into the allocated buffer.  The value UUFFSS__SSTTDDSSBB may be
     specified for _s_b_l_o_c_k_l_o_c to request that the standard location for the su‐
     perblock be read.  The ssbbsseeaarrcchh() function uses the _d_e_v_f_d file descriptor
     that references the filesystem disk, to search first for the superblock
     at the standard location.  If it is not found or is too damaged to use
     ssbbsseeaarrcchh() will attempt to find one of the filesystem's alternate su‐
     perblocks.  Flags are specified by _o_r'ing the following values:

     UUFFSS__NNOOCCSSUUMM  Causes only the superblock itself to be returned, but does
                 not read in any auxiliary data structures like the cylinder
                 group summary information.

     UUFFSS__NNOOMMSSGG   Indicates that superblock inconsistency error messages should
                 not be printed.

     If successful, ssbbggeett() and ssbbsseeaarrcchh() functions return a pointer to the
     buffer containing the superblock in _f_s_p.  The ssbbggeett() and ssbbsseeaarrcchh()
     functions are safe to use in threaded applications.

     The ssbbppuutt() function writes the superblock specified by _f_s to the loca‐
     tion from which it was read on the disk referenced by the _d_e_v_f_d file de‐
     scriptor.  Additionally, the ssbbppuutt() function will update the first
     _n_u_m_a_l_t_w_r_i_t_e alternate superblock locations.  To update all the alternate
     superblocks, specify a _n_u_m_a_l_t_w_r_i_t_e value of _f_s_-_>_f_s___n_c_g.  The ssbbppuutt()
     function is safe to use in threaded applications.  Note that the ssbbppuutt()
     function needs to be called only if the superblock has been modified and
     the on-disk copy needs to be updated.

     The ssbbrreeaadd() function reads the standard filesystem superblock.  The
     ssbbffiinndd() function tries to find a usable superblock.  It searchs first
     for the superblock at the standard location.  If it is not found or is
     too damaged to use ssbbffiinndd() will attempt to find one of the filesystem's
     alternate superblocks.  If successful ssbbrreeaadd() and ssbbffiinndd() return a su‐
     perblock in the _d___s_b, structure embedded in the given user-land UFS disk
     structure.

     The ssbbwwrriittee() function writes the superblock from the _d___s_b, structure em‐
     bedded in the given user-land UFS disk structure to the location from
     which it was read.  Additionally, the ssbbwwrriittee() function will write to
     all the alternate superblock locations if the _a_l_l value is non-zero.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ssbbrreeaadd() and ssbbwwrriittee() functions return the value 0 if successful;
     otherwise the value -1 is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set
     to indicate the error.  The ssbbggeett(), ssbbsseeaarrcchh(), and ssbbppuutt() functions
     return the value 0 if successful; otherwise they return one of the errors
     described below.

EERRRROORRSS
     The errors returned by ssbbggeett(), ssbbsseeaarrcchh(), ssbbrreeaadd(), and ssbbffiinndd(), in‐
     clude any of the errors specified for the library function bread(3).  Ad‐
     ditionally, they may follow the libufs(3) error methodologies in situa‐
     tions where no usable superblock could be found.

     The errors returned by ssbbppuutt() and ssbbwwrriittee() include any of the errors
     specified for the library function bwrite(3).

